@using Concierge.Shared.Design
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IServiceProvider Services
@implements IDisposable

@*
    Design: say it, see it, point at it.

    One surface. A canvas, a strip of everything it has looked like, and six looks
    to choose between — and nothing else. No file name, no layers, no properties
    panel, no timeline, no code view, no colour picker. Every one of those is a
    thing somebody would have to be taught, and the bar this is built to is that a
    five-year-old and a ninety-seven-year-old can both use it without being taught
    anything.

    The composer underneath is unchanged; it is the "say it" half. What you type
    there while this is open goes to the canvas.

    Three things this deliberately does not have:

      A save button. The design is kept as you go and every state it has been in is
      one tap away. Saving is a thing computers needed, not people.

      An "are you sure". Design acts freely — asking permission to try something is
      what makes a tool unusable for the people this is for, and it teaches somebody
      to stop reading prompts, which ruins every approval elsewhere in Concierge.
      What makes that safe is that going back is free and total.

      Anything that scrolls out of sight. Scrollbars are hidden app-wide, so a
      surface that overflows loses content silently.
*@

<div class="dz">
    <div class="dz-top">
        <span class="dz-what">@_session.Moments[_session.Position].What</span>

        <div class="dz-back">
            <button class="dz-step" type="button" disabled="@(!_session.CanGoBack)"
                    @onclick="Back" title="Go back" aria-label="Go back">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                     stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                    <polyline points="15 6 9 12 15 18" />
                </svg>
            </button>
            <button class="dz-step" type="button" disabled="@(!_session.CanGoForward)"
                    @onclick="Forward" title="Go forward" aria-label="Go forward">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                     stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                    <polyline points="9 6 15 12 9 18" />
                </svg>
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    @*
        The canvas. An iframe rather than the design rendered inline, so a look
        can bring its own background, fonts and spacing without any of it leaking
        into Concierge's own stylesheet — or Concierge's leaking into the design,
        which would make what you see here not what you get anywhere else.
    *@
    <div class="dz-canvas">
        @*
            Both flags, and the reasoning is worth having in one place because each
            was got wrong on its own first.

            allow-same-origin is what lets the click listener be attached inside
            the frame. Without it contentDocument is null and pointing cannot work
            at all.

            allow-scripts is what makes four of the five renderers do anything.
            Arrow keys through a deck, a video that plays, a room that turns, a
            running order that plays end to end — all of it is script inside the
            document. Without this the designs still drew perfectly and did
            nothing, which is exactly the sort of thing that passes a test suite
            and fails a person.

            The pair is the combination usually warned about, because framed
            content can then remove its own sandbox. What makes it safe here is
            not the sandbox: it is that the document is generated by our own
            renderer, every piece of text is escaped, and picture and audio
            sources are restricted to http, https and data. There is no path for
            anything to arrive that was not written by this application.
        *@
        <iframe class="dz-frame" title="Your design" sandbox="allow-same-origin allow-scripts"
                srcdoc="@_session.Html()" @ref="_frame"></iframe>

        @if (_touched is not null)
        {
            <div class="dz-touched">
                <span>@_touched</span>
                <button class="dz-untouch" type="button" @onclick="() => Point(null)">Let go</button>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    @*
        Everything it has looked like, as pictures. Not a list of changes — "Set
        node_4f2a text" is unreadable, and a person deciding which version they
        liked is looking for the one that looked right, not the one described
        correctly.
    *@
    <div class="dz-past" role="group" aria-label="How it looked before">
        @for (var i = 0; i < _session.Moments.Count; i++)
        {
            var at = i;
            var moment = _session.Moments[at];

            <button class="dz-moment @(at == _session.Position ? "is-now" : string.Empty)"
                    type="button" @onclick="() => GoTo(at)" title="@moment.What">
                <span class="dz-thumb">
                    <iframe class="dz-thumb-frame" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"
                            sandbox="" srcdoc="@_session.HtmlAt(at)"></iframe>
                </span>
                <span class="dz-moment-what">@moment.What</span>
            </button>
        }
    </div>

    @*
        What is being made. Five, chosen by pointing at one.

        Above the looks rather than below, because it is the bigger question: what
        this is decides what "add one" even means — a slide, a shot, a room, a
        track. The word under each is the one a person would use, so nobody has to
        learn that a deck is called a deck.
    *@
    <div class="dz-looks dz-mediums" role="group" aria-label="What you are making">
        @foreach (var medium in DesignMediums.All)
        {
            var pick = medium;

            <button class="dz-look @(_session.Current.Medium == pick.Medium ? "is-on" : string.Empty)"
                    type="button" @onclick="() => Make(pick.Medium)" title="@pick.Blurb">
                <span class="dz-kind" aria-hidden="true">@Glyph(pick.Medium)</span>
                <span class="dz-look-name">@pick.Name</span>
            </button>
        }
    </div>

    @*
        How it should look, chosen by pointing at one. Never a colour, never a
        font name, never a file — six pictures and a word.
    *@
    <div class="dz-looks" role="group" aria-label="How it should look">
        @foreach (var look in DesignLooks.All)
        {
            var name = look.Name;

            <button class="dz-look @(string.Equals(_session.Current.Look, name, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase) ? "is-on" : string.Empty)"
                    type="button" @onclick="() => Wear(name)" title="@look.Blurb">
                <span class="dz-swatch" style="background:@look.Ground;border-color:@look.Raised">
                    <span class="dz-swatch-ink" style="background:@look.Ink"></span>
                    <span class="dz-swatch-accent" style="background:@look.Accent"></span>
                </span>
                <span class="dz-look-name">@name</span>
            </button>
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The design being worked on. Owned by the workspace, so it survives
    /// this component being torn down and rebuilt by a resize.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public DesignSession Session { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Told when something changed, so the workspace can redraw around it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback Changed { get; set; }

    private DesignSession _session = default!;
    private ElementReference _frame;
    private string? _touched;
    private DotNetObjectReference<Design>? _self;
    private bool _wired;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (ReferenceEquals(_session, Session))
        {
            return;
        }

        if (_session is not null)
        {
            _session.Changed -= OnSessionChanged;
        }

        _session = Session;
        _session.Changed += OnSessionChanged;
        Describe();
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        // Clicks land inside the iframe, so the listener has to be attached in
        // there. The document is same-origin (srcdoc), which is what makes this
        // possible at all — and is why the canvas is srcdoc rather than a URL.
        if (!_wired)
        {
            _self = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);

            try
            {
                await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("conciergeDesign.watch", _frame, _self);
                _wired = true;
            }
            catch (JSException)
            {
                // Pointing is the better way to correct something and not the only
                // one — every sentence still works. A canvas you cannot click is
                // worse than one you can; a canvas that throws on load is useless.
            }
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Something on the canvas was touched. Called from the page inside the frame.
    /// </summary>
    [JSInvokable]
    public void Touched(string? nodeId)
    {
        Point(nodeId);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// How the room got drawn. Called from the page inside the frame.
    ///
    /// A room that falls back to the flat drawing works perfectly, which is the
    /// whole point of drawing it that way — and is exactly why nobody would ever
    /// find out it had. Engineering says which, so "why does my room look flat?"
    /// has an answer that is not a guess.
    /// </summary>
    [JSInvokable]
    public void Drew(bool engine, string? why)
    {
        if (Services.GetService(typeof(SceneEngineReport)) is not SceneEngineReport report)
        {
            // A head that does not keep the report keeps its canvas. This is a
            // thing Engineering shows, not a thing the canvas needs.
            return;
        }

        if (engine)
        {
            report.Drew();
        }
        else
        {
            report.FellBack(why);
        }
    }

    private void Point(string? nodeId)
    {
        _session.Point(nodeId);
        Describe();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private void Back()
    {
        _session.Back();
        Describe();
    }

    private void Forward()
    {
        _session.Forward();
        Describe();
    }

    private void GoTo(int position)
    {
        _session.GoTo(position);
        Describe();
    }

    private void Wear(string look)
    {
        _session.Record(_session.Current.Wearing(look), $"Made it {look.ToLowerInvariant()}");
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Changes what is being made. Nothing on the canvas is thrown away — the same
    /// words and pictures, drawn a different way — so changing your mind costs
    /// nothing and is one tap back regardless.
    /// </summary>
    private void Make(DesignMedium medium)
    {
        if (_session.Current.Medium == medium)
        {
            return;
        }

        _session.Record(
            _session.Current.As(medium),
            $"Made it {DesignMediums.Of(medium).Name.ToLowerInvariant()}");
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A shape for each medium. Drawn rather than named because the row has to
    /// read at a glance on a phone, where the words are hidden.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Glyph(DesignMedium medium) => medium switch
    {
        DesignMedium.Deck => "▤",
        DesignMedium.Motion => "▶",
        DesignMedium.Scene => "◳",
        DesignMedium.Sound => "♫",
        _ => "▭",
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// What is being pointed at, in the words a person would use. "The title" and
    /// not "heading#4f2a1c" — this is read at a glance while deciding what to say
    /// next.
    ///
    /// **Every kind is named.** Three were missing and all three fell through to
    /// "The page": pointing at a block in a room, a track in a running order, or a
    /// slide said the page had been touched, and the very next sentence was then
    /// about the wrong thing. Caught by clicking a box in a room and reading the
    /// chip — the same class of defect as an approvals badge that always said two,
    /// on the one strip whose whole job is saying what you just touched.
    ///
    /// A frame borrows the medium's own word, so it is a slide on a deck, a shot
    /// in a film and a room in a space.
    /// </summary>
    private void Describe()
    {
        _touched = _session.Pointed is { } node
            ? node.Kind == DesignNodeKind.Page
                ? "The page"
                : $"This {DesignSpeech.NameFor(node.Kind, _session.Current.Medium)}"
            : null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The session changed, possibly from a thread that is not the renderer's.
    ///
    /// **This killed the whole app.** `await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged)` marshals that one
    /// call correctly — and then the continuation resumes on whatever thread raised the
    /// event, so `Changed.InvokeAsync()` reached the workspace's own StateHasChanged off the
    /// dispatcher and threw "The current thread is not associated with the Dispatcher". In an
    /// `async void` that is an unhandled exception on a thread-pool thread, which does not
    /// fail the edit — it takes the process down. Concierge vanished from the screen twice
    /// before the trace was captured.
    ///
    /// It only shows when a tool does real asynchronous work before recording: reading files
    /// off a disk, fetching a sound, generating a picture, speaking words. Everything that
    /// records synchronously stays on the dispatcher by luck, which is why a surface this old
    /// had never done it.
    ///
    /// Everything is inside one `InvokeAsync` now, so the whole handler — including the
    /// parent's callback — runs where the renderer lives. And it is no longer `async void`,
    /// so a future fault in here costs the update rather than the application.
    /// </summary>
    private void OnSessionChanged(object? sender, EventArgs e)
        => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
        {
            Describe();
            StateHasChanged();
            await Changed.InvokeAsync();
        });

    public void Dispose()
    {
        if (_session is not null)
        {
            _session.Changed -= OnSessionChanged;
        }

        _self?.Dispose();
    }
}
